<p>Two different failures wait here and they come from different classes. With either value missing — the
guard is an <code>or</code>, not an <code>and</code> — encoding throws <code>Pop\Db\Record\Exception</code>,
<code>Error: The encryption properties have not been set.</code> With both present but the key not valid base64
for the cipher, it's <code>Pop\Crypt\Encryption\Exception</code>,
<code>Error: Invalid key or unsupported cipher.</code></p>
<p><code>APP_PREVIOUS_KEYS</code> holds a comma-separated list of retired keys, so decryption keeps working through a
rotation while new writes use the current one. Every entry is base64 like <code>APP_KEY</code>.</p>
<p><code>APP_PREVIOUS_KEYS</code> is read alongside <code>APP_KEY</code>, so a class that sets <code>$key</code> itself supplies its own
previous keys too.</p>
<p>Hashed columns are checked with <code>verify()</code>, which takes the column name and the attempted plaintext:</p>
